Procedure
Step 1: Log in to AWS Console
Open AWS Management Console.
Go to Amazon Web Services EC2 Dashboard.
Click Launch Instance.
Step 2: Configure Linux EC2 Instance
Enter instance name.
Choose an Ubuntu/Linux AMI.
Select instance type (t2.micro for Free Tier).
Create or select a key pair.
Download the .pem key file safely.
Under Network Settings:
Allow SSH traffic
Port: 22
Source: My IP
Click Launch Instance.
Step 3: Verify Security Group
Go to:
EC2 → Security Groups
Open inbound rules.
Ensure the following rule exists:
Type	Protocol	Port	Source
SSH	TCP	22	My IP
Step 4: Install PuTTY and PuTTYgen
Download and install:
PuTTY
PuTTYgen
Open PuTTYgen.
Step 5: Convert .pem File to .ppk
In PuTTYgen click Load.
Change file type to All Files.
Select the downloaded .pem key.
Click Open.
Click Save Private Key.
Save file as .ppk.
Step 6: Connect Using PuTTY
Open PuTTY.
In Session:
Enter EC2 Public DNS/IP.
Navigate:
Connection → SSH → Auth → Credentials
Click Browse.
Select .ppk file.
Click Open.
Step 7: Login to EC2
Accept security warning.
Login username:
ubuntu (Ubuntu)
ec2-user (Amazon Linux)
Terminal opens successfully.
Step 8: Verify Connection

Run:

whoami

Expected Output:

ubuntu
Observation
EC2 instance launched successfully.
Security group allowed SSH access on port 22.
.pem key converted to .ppk.
PuTTY established secure SSH connection.
Linux terminal accessed successfully.
Result

Successfully created a Linux EC2 instance and connected securely using PuTTY and SSH authentication.
